    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 091927\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1227 PM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Elevated flows will continue on the Salt and Gila Rivers through at\n  least the middle of the week due to recent releases from \n  upstream dams.\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather with slightly above normal temperatures and\n  some occasional breeziness are expected through middle of the \n  week.\n\n- A more unsettled pattern with increasing rain chances is\n  possible over the weekend into next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nA benign weather pattern is expected across the Desert Southwest \nthrough the middle of the week as an overall ridge of high pressure \nprevails overhead. This will produce dry and tranquil conditions \nalong with mostly clear skies. Temperatures will be in an above \nnormal category each day with afternoon highs across the lower \ndeserts topping out in the low to mid 80s along with overnight \nlows in the 50s to around 60 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nAn amplifying upper level pattern late week suggests a Pacific \ntrough will develop off the West Coast by around Thursday with a \nsmall majority of latest runs' ensemble members forming a closed \nlow just off the coast of southern/central California by Friday. \nOther members have more of a faster and less amplified disturbance\nwhich could still occur, but is a bit less likely to happen. For \nnow with the majority of the members, including the deterministic \nmembers, calling for a closed low to approach the region on \nFriday, higher level clouds will be the first feature to move in \nfrom the southwest.\n\nThe latest model trends however lean more toward the low tracking\nacross northern Baja into Sonora Mexico Friday night and \nSaturday. If this track holds, we are not likely to get much lower\nlevel moisture to advect northward, along with the bulk of the \nsystem energy missing us to the south. The latest NBM/WPC PoPs \nhave been scaled back due to the potential more southerly track \nleaving mostly 10-20% chances over the lower deserts and up to 30%\nover Arizona higher terrain areas Friday night-Saturday night. \nAny further weakening of the system or farther track to the south \nwould decrease rain chances. Despite the uncertainty in rain \nchances, there is higher confidence in a cooler air mass moving \ninto our region for next weekend. Current NBM forecast \ntemperatures show highs dropping into the mid to upper 70s\nstarting Saturday. Even if this first system ends up not \nproviding any meaningful rainfall, guidance is hinting at one or \neven two additional weather systems for next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1740Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major weather issues will exist through Tuesday afternoon. An\neasterly wind component will prevail the majority of the period.\nHowever, winds may become very light and VRB for a few hours\nbefore and after sunset. Confidence is low that winds will become\ndefinitively west at any point during the TAF period. Overnight\ninto early Tuesday morning, assuming the surface is sufficiently\ndecoupled, we could see minor LLWS concerns (below TAF criteria) \non the order of 10-15 kts out of the east. Clear skies will give \nway to FEW- SCT high cirrus on Tuesday.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Tuesday morning under clear\nskies today, followed by increasing high cirrus tonight. Winds \ntrends will be similar to the past 24 hours favoring a west \ncomponent at KIPL and north directions at KBLH, albeit with \nprolonged periods of nearly calm conditions. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions and slightly above normal temperatures will \nprevail for the bulk of the coming week. Minimum humidity values \nwill hover near 25-40% across eastern districts and 20-30% in \nwestern districts. This follows fair to good overnight recovery \nnear 30-60% in western areas and good to excellent levels 50-80% \nin eastern areas. Outside of some marginally breezy conditions \nacross ridge tops and terrain gaps of eastern districts during \nthe morning hours, fairly light winds will prevail through the\nmiddle of the week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Whittock/18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
